Accountability exists to protect the client experience, the salon culture, company standards, and fairness across the team. Accountability is not about being harsh. It is about being clear, consistent, and timely.

A strong Sun Tan City leader holds employees accountable when:
- expectations have been communicated;
- the employee has been trained or coached;
- the behavior affects clients, revenue, standards, safety, attendance, dress code, or team trust;
- the same issue repeats after follow-up;
- an employee chooses not to follow policy, direction, or expected behavior.

Accountability should be documented when a behavior needs to be corrected, monitored, or escalated. The documentation should preserve facts, not feelings.

A Sun Tan City manager is responsible for creating clarity, not confusion. The manager must not assume that employees know what to do simply because a metric is low or a policy exists. The manager must inspect, teach, reinforce, and hold accountable.

Manager responsibilities include:
- observe client interactions, not just reports;
- review Daily Stats, Bonus Viewer, Comp Report, productivity tracking, shift summaries, and coaching history;
- identify the behavior causing the metric or issue;
- coach in the moment whenever possible;
- use role-play to build skill;
- document coaching when a pattern or opportunity exists;
- use follow-up notes to prove whether behavior improved;
- praise publicly when appropriate and correct privately;
- avoid emotional decisions;
- communicate policies and priorities positively;
- never vent downward to employees;
- ask for support from the DM or appropriate leader when unsure.

A manager should not simply say, "Get your Spa % up," "Sell more clubs," or "Do better." Those statements do not teach behavior. A strong manager says, "Today I need you to recommend Spa Wellness to every eligible client by asking a wellness-based question, demonstrating how it supports their goal, and planting the seed if they do not buy today. I will observe your next two client interactions and we will debrief after."

Employees are responsible for participating in their own improvement. Coaching is a two-way process. The manager must teach and follow up, and the employee must apply the expectation.

Employee responsibilities include:
- listen to coaching with professionalism;
- ask questions if expectations are unclear;
- participate in role-play and practice;
- use required tools such as BHOW, ASTC, Routine Mat, and client engagement questions;
- follow MSB and CES consistently;
- follow attendance, dress code, safety, and company policy expectations;
- demonstrate improvement after coaching;
- communicate barriers early;
- take ownership of client interactions and productivity results.

Ask Bubbles should hold both sides accountable: managers must coach clearly and follow up, and employees must demonstrate behavior change.

When it should be used:
- whenever a manager notices a performance gap, behavior concern, client engagement opportunity, or metric concern;
- before writing documentation when the manager needs facts;
- while reviewing reports, live interactions, video where applicable, shift summaries, or salon operations.

Why it should be used:
- facts prevent emotional or vague coaching;
- live observation helps identify the actual behavior causing the issue;
- reports show what is happening, but observation explains why it is happening.

Appropriate documentation:
- manager notes;
- shift summary;
- Daily Stats audit notes;
- observed interaction notes;
- coaching planner notes.

Expected manager actions:
- record what happened in objective terms;
- avoid labels such as lazy, rude, negative, or careless unless tied to specific observable behavior;
- identify whether the issue is skill, knowledge, confidence, effort, or policy;
- decide whether immediate coaching is needed.

Reusable observation statement:
"During observed client interactions, [Employee] did not [expected behavior]. The client was checked in without [tool/process/recommendation]. This impacts [client experience/revenue/standard]."

When it should be used:
- when the employee needs correction, clarity, training, or re-training;
- when a report shows underperformance and the manager can identify a behavior to coach;
- when the employee misses MSB, CES, ASTC, BHOW, Routine Mat, relevant recommendations, tours, re-tours, closing, or planting the seed;
- when a manager sees a single-topic coaching opportunity.

Why it should be used:
- coaching gives employees a chance to improve before escalation;
- it builds consistent client engagement habits;
- it creates documentation that support was given.

Appropriate documentation:
- Coaching Form;
- coaching planner entry;
- role-play evaluation if practice occurred;
- follow-up note after observation.

Expected manager actions:
- state the observed behavior;
- explain why it matters;
- demonstrate the correct behavior or verbiage;
- require practice;
- set a follow-up expectation;
- document the employee’s acknowledgement.

Reusable coaching statement:
"Today we are coaching [topic]. I observed [specific behavior]. The expectation is [specific behavior]. This matters because [impact]. We practiced [scenario/verbiage]. I will follow up by [date/shift/interaction count]."

When it should be used:
- when the employee knows the expectation but struggles with words, confidence, objections, or execution;
- after coaching on memberships, Spa, Routine Mat, ASTC, BHOW, tours, re-tours, product recommendations, or difficult client situations;
- when the same skill gap appears more than once;
- as part of an EPP or follow-up coaching plan.

Why it should be used:
- role-play builds muscle memory;
- employees need to practice under pressure before live client interactions;
- realistic objections help employees learn how to stay confident without sounding pushy;
- role-play lets the manager verify understanding before expecting live performance.

Appropriate documentation:
- Management Diamond;
- role-play evaluation form;
- Coaching Form notes;
- EPP task completion notes.

Expected manager actions:
- make role-play realistic, not easy;
- make it safe and supportive;
- demonstrate first, then have the employee practice;
- use common client types and real objections;
- include management and consultants when appropriate;
- debrief with kindness and support;
- document strengths and weaknesses;
- assign follow-up practice.

Reusable role-play statement:
"We practiced [scenario]. Strengths observed: [strengths]. Improvement needed: [specific improvement]. Next practice will focus on [next behavior]."

When it should be used:
- after any documented coaching;
- after role-play;
- when the manager said they would observe a future interaction;
- when an employee partially improves but still needs reinforcement;
- when deciding whether to continue coaching or escalate.

Why it should be used:
- coaching without follow-up becomes a suggestion instead of an expectation;
- follow-up shows whether behavior changed;
- documentation must show progress, not just the original issue.

Appropriate documentation:
- follow-up coaching note;
- updated Coaching Form;
- shift summary;
- EPP follow-up entry;
- manager-to-DM summary.

Expected manager actions:
- observe the same behavior again;
- praise specific improvement;
- correct remaining gaps;
- document whether expectations were met;
- set next action: continue, adjust, EPP, DPOA, or leadership review.

Reusable follow-up statement:
"Follow-up from prior coaching on [topic]: [Employee] demonstrated [improvement] during [observation]. Continued focus is [remaining gap]. Next expectation is [specific behavior]."

When it should be used:
- when an employee has repeated underperformance that requires a formal improvement plan;
- when coaching and role-play have not produced enough improvement;
- when an employee is underperforming without visible improvement efforts;
- when a management employee needs structured development in leadership, accountability, productivity, bench, training, or salon execution;
- when a role transition requires clear expectations and development milestones.

Why it should be used:
- EPPs organize expectations, strengths, opportunities, goals, and follow-up into one formal plan;
- they give the employee a clear path to improvement;
- they help the manager document support, accountability, and results.

Appropriate documentation:
- EPP form;
- productivity history summary;
- prior coaching documentation;
- role-play and follow-up notes;
- self-reflection from employee;
- re-evaluation notes.

Expected manager actions:
- review role expectations;
- list strengths and improvement opportunities;
- include relevant performance trends without dumping raw reports;
- create behavior-based plan of action;
- define measurable goals and timeline;
- schedule follow-up and re-evaluation;
- document employee acknowledgement;
- update the plan after re-evaluation.

Reusable EPP statement:
"This EPP is being created because [Employee] has not consistently demonstrated [performance expectation] despite coaching and support. The goal of the plan is to build consistent execution of [behaviors] and improve [metrics/outcomes] by [re-evaluation point]."

When it should be used:
- during the scheduled EPP follow-up;
- when the plan timeline reaches a checkpoint;
- when deciding whether the employee has met, partially met, or not met objectives.

Why it should be used:
- the manager must close the loop on the plan;
- it prevents vague or endless improvement plans;
- it creates a factual basis for next steps.

Appropriate documentation:
- EPP re-evaluation section;
- progress update;
- coaching follow-up notes;
- leadership summary if escalation is needed.

Expected manager actions:
- review each objective;
- identify what was met and not met;
- include evidence from observations and metrics;
- decide whether to complete the plan, extend/adjust the plan, move to DPOA, or escalate.

Reusable follow-up review statement:
"At this review, [Employee] met [objectives] and did not meet [objectives]. Evidence reviewed includes [observations/metrics/coaching completion]. Next action is [complete/continue/adjust/escalate]."

When it should be used:
- when an employee violates policy;
- when the issue is attendance, tardiness, leaving early, dress code, standards of conduct, or company policy;
- when the employee refuses or fails to follow management direction;
- when underperformance continues after coaching/EPP and requires formal warning;
- when the issue is serious enough to require immediate accountability.

Why it should be used:
- DPOA documents formal warning and accountability;
- it connects the observed behavior to the policy or job requirement;
- it creates a clear action plan and consequence.

Appropriate documentation:
- DPOA form;
- exact policy reference from the Driven to Shine manual or applicable current company policy;
- prior coaching/EPP if relevant;
- factual observation;
- action plan;
- witness signature when required.

Expected manager actions:
- document the observation objectively;
- cite the direct policy or expectation;
- administer privately;
- avoid emotional or aggressive language;
- explain action plan and consequences;
- provide employee copy as required by process;
- consult DM/HR/LP where needed.

Reusable DPOA statement:
"This DPOA is being issued because [Employee] [specific behavior], which violates [policy/expectation]. The expectation going forward is [specific action]. Any further violation may result in additional disciplinary action, up to and including termination."

When it should be used:
- when termination, demotion, suspension, or final disciplinary action is being considered;
- when the issue involves protected categories, leave, harassment, discrimination, safety complaints, retaliation concerns, theft, fraud, client account manipulation, payroll abuse, or other sensitive matters;
- when the manager is unsure whether coaching, EPP, or DPOA is appropriate;
- when the employee has multiple documents and no improvement.

Why it should be used:
- serious employment decisions require consistency and leadership review;
- escalation protects the employee, the manager, the salon, and the company;
- managers should not make emotional or unsupported decisions.

Appropriate documentation:
- summary of history;
- copies of coaching, role-play, EPP, DPOA, and follow-up notes;
- factual timeline;
- policy references;
- leadership recommendation.

Expected manager actions:
- gather documents;
- summarize facts;
- avoid assumptions;
- contact DM or appropriate leadership before proceeding;
- follow current company procedure.

Expectations should be simple, specific, and repeatable.

A strong expectation includes:
- the client type or situation;
- the required behavior;
- the required tool or verbiage;
- the frequency;
- the follow-up method.

Example:
"For every eligible client today, I need you to use at least one open-ended question, make a relevant recommendation based on their answer, use ASTC if they hesitate, and either complete the engagement or plant the seed. I will observe two interactions before the end of your shift."

Ask Bubbles should recommend an EPP when the issue requires a formal improvement or development plan rather than a single coaching form.

Common EPP triggers:
- repeated underperformance after coaching;
- employee is on an underperformance list or consistently missing expectations;
- employee is not showing improvement efforts;
- productivity remains low because required behaviors are not being demonstrated;
- employee is moving into a leadership role and needs a structured development plan;
- ASD, SDIT, SD, TSD, or DMIT needs documented leadership development;
- leadership behaviors such as delegation, accountability, global awareness, autonomy, time management, bench development, training, or follow-up are not consistent;
- a manager is not coaching, role-playing, or holding the team accountable;
- salon results are affected by weak execution, training, bench, or leadership routines.

An EPP should not be used as a substitute for a DPOA when the issue is clearly a policy violation or serious misconduct. When the issue is both performance and policy-related, Ask Bubbles should recommend leadership review before choosing the form.

Common EPP timelines:
- 30 days for most structured performance improvement plans;
- shorter if the issue is urgent and leadership approves;
- longer if the plan is developmental and tied to role progression;
- weekly follow-up for formal plans;
- per-shift follow-up for counter behavior, coaching, or role-play expectations.

Ask Bubbles should not create an EPP without a follow-up point. An EPP without follow-up is not a performance plan.

Accountability should escalate when coaching is not enough, when a policy is violated, or when the behavior is serious or repeated.

Escalation indicators:
- repeated issue after coaching and follow-up;
- employee does not make improvement efforts;
- employee refuses role-play or coaching;
- employee fails to follow management direction;
- policy violation occurs;
- behavior affects safety, client trust, money, inventory, payroll, confidentiality, or company reputation;
- attendance or dress code issue repeats;
- employee’s behavior undermines salon culture or standards.

Ask Bubbles should recommend DPOA or leadership review when the issue is no longer just a skill gap.

A DPOA should be used for formal warning or disciplinary action. It may be verbal warning, written warning, termination, or demotion depending on severity, prior documentation, and leadership direction.

Typical DPOA categories:
- tardiness or leaving early;
- absenteeism;
- standards of conduct;
- under performance requiring formal warning;
- dress code violation;
- violation of company policies;
- failure to follow management direction;
- other documented policy or conduct issue.

Coaching teaches.
EPP structures improvement.
DPOA documents formal accountability.

Use coaching when:
- the employee needs skill development;
- the issue is correctable through demonstration and practice;
- the employee has not yet received clear coaching on the expectation.

Use EPP when:
- the issue is repeated performance underperformance;
- the employee needs a formal plan with goals and review;
- a leader needs structured development or correction.

Use DPOA when:
- a policy is violated;
- behavior is unacceptable or repeated;
- coaching/EPP did not lead to improvement;
- the issue requires a formal warning or consequence.

A DPOA must include:
- employee information;
- type of warning;
- whether prior discipline exists for the same or related issue;
- type of offense;
- observation of offense;
- exact policy violated or job expectation violated;
- action plan;
- consequence language;
- signatures and witness when required.

Observation of offense should be factual:
"[Employee] arrived [amount of time] after scheduled start time on [date/timeframe] without following call-in procedure."

Policy violated should reference current company policy:
"[Policy title/section] from the Driven to Shine manual or current applicable policy."

Action plan should be specific:
"Going forward, [Employee] must [specific required behavior]. Any further violation may result in additional disciplinary action, up to and including termination."

Ask Bubbles should not invent a policy title or manual page if the manager does not provide it. Instead, ask for or recommend verifying the exact policy reference.

This section teaches Ask Bubbles how to translate common Sun Tan City issues into coaching, follow-up, and escalation recommendations.

Likely causes:
- employees are not recommending Spa Wellness to every eligible client;
- Spa is treated as optional instead of part of the client’s overall experience;
- employees do not know how to connect Spa to client goals;
- employees do not demonstrate Spa or related products;
- managers are not following up after missed Spa opportunities;
- employees are avoiding Spa because they are uncomfortable explaining it.

Coaching focus:
- recommend Spa consistently;
- ask wellness-based or results-based questions;
- connect Spa to what the client said they want;
- demonstrate or describe the experience confidently;
- plant the seed even when the client does not buy;
- use Spa as a bridge to relevant product recommendations when appropriate.

Suggested coaching conversation:
"I observed that Spa Wellness was not being recommended consistently. Spa should be part of our client engagement because it helps us educate clients and connect them to services that fit their goals. I want you to ask one wellness-based question and recommend Spa to every eligible client today. Let’s practice how you would bring it up with a client who says they only came in to tan."

Follow-up expectations:
- observe two eligible client interactions;
- review Spa recommendations during shift summary;
- document if Spa was recommended and whether ASTC or planting the seed occurred.

Let’s practice how you would bring it up with a client who says they only came in to tan."

Follow-up expectations:
- observe two eligible client interactions;
- review Spa recommendations during shift summary;
- document if Spa was recommended and whether ASTC or planting the seed occurred.

Escalation recommendations:
- first occurrence: coaching and role-play;
- repeated after coaching: follow-up coaching and Management Diamond;
- ongoing refusal or no improvement: EPP or DPOA depending on history and effort.

Likely causes:
- employees are not presenting memberships to every opportunity;
- employees lead with the lowest-cost option instead of showing value top-down;
- BHOW is not being used;
- employees stop after the first hesitation;
- employees treat hesitation as a final no;
- weak urgency during promotions or events;
- managers are not listening to membership presentations.

Coaching focus:
- re-tour when appropriate;
- use BHOW to show membership value;
- present the best-fit membership confidently;
- use ASTC for hesitations;
- complete the engagement or plant the seed;
- explain benefits without using alarming language.

Suggested coaching conversation:
"Your opportunity is not just asking if the client wants a membership. It is showing the client why the membership fits their needs. Today I need you to present the best-fit membership using BHOW, then use ASTC if they hesitate. Let’s role-play a client who says they only want one visit."

Follow-up expectations:
- manager observes membership presentations;
- track whether BHOW and ASTC were used;
- review closing language after each observed interaction.

Escalation recommendations:
- coaching and role-play for skill gap;
- Management Diamond if repeated;
- EPP if Club Close remains low because required behaviors are not used;
- DPOA if employee refuses to follow membership presentation expectations.

Likely causes:
- employees are not asking enough open-ended questions;
- recommendations are not relevant to client needs;
- Routine Mat is not used;
- employees recommend one item instead of a complete routine;
- employees fail to demonstrate products;
- employees do not close or plant the seed;
- managers are not coaching product conversations.

Coaching focus:
- ask results-based questions;
- use Routine Mat as a full-routine guide;
- explain what the product is and what it does;
- recommend based on the client’s stated need;
- ask for the sale;
- plant the seed if the client does not purchase.

Suggested coaching conversation:
"Your PPTA opportunity is connected to the way you are educating clients. I need you to use the Routine Mat during product conversations, ask what the client wants from their results, and recommend a complete routine based on what they tell you. Let’s practice turning one client answer into a relevant recommendation."

Follow-up expectations:
- observe product conversations;
- check whether Routine Mat was used;
- debrief verbiage and relevance;
- review Daily Stats for behavior trend, not just number.

Escalation recommendations:
- coaching and role-play first;
- EPP if ongoing underperformance continues after support;
- DPOA if employee refuses required tools or coaching.

Likely causes:
- weak product recommendations;
- low PPTA behaviors;
- employees not using Routine Mat;
- not demonstrating products;
- not tying products to tanning results;
- missed promo/event urgency;
- no follow-up after hesitation;
- manager not coaching OTC opportunities daily.

Coaching focus:
- connect OTC to client results;
- use Routine Mat and full-routine recommendation;
- show value, not just price;
- demonstrate product;
- use ASTC for price hesitations;
- role-play top objections.

Suggested coaching conversation:
"OTC improves when we educate clients on how to get the results they came in for. I observed product conversations ending without a full routine recommendation. Today I need you to use the Routine Mat, explain what each recommendation does, and ask for the sale. If the client hesitates on price, use ASTC and plant the seed."

Follow-up expectations:
- observe product presentations;
- review OTC opportunities from Daily Stats;
- document which behaviors improved.

Escalation recommendations:
- if skill issue: coaching and role-play;
- if repeated and metrics remain low: EPP;
- if employee avoids product recommendations after coaching: formal accountability may be appropriate.

Likely causes:
- employee is task-focused instead of client-focused;
- employee uses closed-ended questions;
- employee does not build rapport;
- employee skips greeting, exit, eyewear, tour, re-tour, or wait-time communication;
- employee is uncomfortable transitioning from casual talk to tan talk;
- manager is not modeling client engagement.

Coaching focus:
- friendly greeting and exit;
- open-ended questions;
- results-based questions;
- rapport building;
- transition from non-tan talk to tan talk;
- relevant recommendations;
- Red Carpet service behaviors;
- completing or planting the seed.

Suggested coaching conversation:
"Client engagement should feel warm, but it also has to lead to education. I observed that the conversation stayed friendly but did not transition into tanning questions or recommendations. I need you to ask one open-ended tanning question, connect the answer to a recommendation, and complete the engagement. Let’s practice that transition."

Follow-up expectations:
- observe live interaction;
- debrief after client leaves;
- document whether employee asked open-ended questions and made a relevant recommendation.

Escalation recommendations:
- coach and role-play first;
- EPP if poor engagement is repeated and affects productivity;
- DPOA if behavior becomes disrespectful or violates standards.

Likely causes:
- employee hears hesitation as rejection;
- employee is afraid of sounding pushy;
- employee does not know support statements;
- employee skips temperature check;
- employee does not ask for the sale;
- employee has not practiced objections under pressure.

Coaching focus:
- hesitation is engagement, not rejection;
- acknowledge the concern;
- support with facts, benefits, value, and reassurance;
- temperature check;
- complete engagement or plant seed;
- practice top objections.

Suggested coaching conversation:
"When a client hesitates, that is your cue to educate, not end the conversation. I need you to use ASTC every time a client hesitates. Let’s practice a client who says they do not want automatic billing, then a client who says the product is too expensive."

Follow-up expectations:
- manager listens for each ASTC step;
- employee repeats verbiage until confident;
- document whether ASTC was used live.

Escalation recommendations:
- repeated failure after role-play: Management Diamond;
- continued underperformance: EPP;
- refusal to use ASTC: DPOA or leadership review depending on history.

Likely causes:
- employee sees Routine Mat as extra work;
- employee does not know product categories;
- employee recommends from memory instead of client need;
- employee is uncomfortable walking client through a full routine;
- manager has not demonstrated Routine Mat use.

Coaching focus:
- use Routine Mat as a guide, not a script;
- ask questions first;
- connect each recommendation to client’s answer;
- explain what it is and what it does;
- recommend complete routine;
- close or plant seed.

Suggested coaching conversation:
"The Routine Mat helps us educate clients instead of guessing. I need you to use it during product conversations so the client can see the full routine. Let’s practice with a client who says they have dry skin and wants faster results."

Follow-up expectations:
- observe Routine Mat use;
- inspect whether employee is making full-routine recommendations;
- review PPTA/OTC trends after behavior coaching.

Escalation recommendations:
- first: coaching, demonstration, role-play;
- repeated: Management Diamond and follow-up coaching;
- ongoing after support: EPP;
- refusal: DPOA.

Likely causes:
- employee does not understand schedule expectations;
- poor personal planning;
- repeated tardiness or leaving early;
- failure to follow call-in process;
- availability does not match salon needs.

Coaching focus:
- clarify schedule and attendance expectations;
- explain impact on coverage, client service, labor, and team trust;
- confirm employee understands call-in and schedule procedures;
- document if pattern exists.

Suggested conversation:
"Your attendance affects the entire shift because the team is counting on coverage. Going forward, you are expected to report to work as scheduled and follow the proper communication process if an issue occurs."

Follow-up expectations:
- monitor next scheduled shifts;
- document compliance or additional issues.

Escalation recommendations:
- attendance issues are often DPOA issues, especially when repeated or policy-based;
- use exact current attendance policy;
- escalate to DM/HR for repeated issues or complex circumstances.

Likely causes:
- employee does not understand dress code;
- employee forgot required item;
- employee believes the standard is optional;
- manager has not consistently enforced dress code.

Coaching focus:
- explain dress code standard;
- explain professional appearance and brand impact;
- require immediate correction when appropriate;
- set expectation for every future shift.

Suggested conversation:
"Dress code is part of our professional standard and must be followed every shift. Today you are not meeting the expectation because [specific issue]. Going forward, you must arrive in full dress code before clocking in."

Follow-up expectations:
- check next shifts for compliance;
- document repeated violations.

Escalation recommendations:
- dress code is normally handled through coaching or DPOA depending on history and policy;
- repeated violations should not remain informal.

Likely causes:
- employee did not know policy;
- employee ignored policy;
- manager has not consistently reinforced policy;
- employee made a poor judgment decision;
- employee intentionally violated a standard.

Coaching focus:
- if truly a knowledge gap, retrain and document;
- if violation is conduct-related, use DPOA;
- always reference exact current policy.

Suggested conversation:
"This conversation is about a policy expectation. The behavior observed was [behavior]. The policy expectation is [policy]. Going forward, you must [specific action]."

Follow-up expectations:
- verify policy compliance;
- document any repeated issue;
- escalate sensitive or serious issues.

Escalation recommendations:
- many policy violations require DPOA or leadership review rather than coaching only;
- serious issues may bypass earlier ladder steps;
- never guess: verify current policy and leadership process.

Likely causes:
- employee did not understand direction;
- employee chose not to complete task;
- employee disagreed with direction but did not communicate appropriately;
- employee does not respect chain of command;
- manager gave unclear expectations.

Coaching focus:
- clarify instruction;
- ask what prevented completion;
- restate expectation;
- explain impact;
- document if refusal or repeated failure.

Suggested conversation:
"You were directed to [task/behavior], and it was not completed. If a direction is unclear, I need you to ask questions immediately. Going forward, when management gives direction, you are expected to follow it or communicate a barrier before the deadline."

Follow-up expectations:
- assign a clear next action;
- verify completion;
- document whether direction was followed.

Escalation recommendations:
- if misunderstanding: coaching;
- if repeated: DPOA;
- if refusal/insubordination: DPOA or leadership review.

Follow-up documentation proves whether coaching worked. Ask Bubbles should treat follow-up as a required part of performance management, not an optional extra.

Follow-up documentation should capture:
- original coaching topic;
- expectation that was set;
- what was observed after coaching;
- progress made;
- remaining gap;
- next action;
- manager support;
- employee response when relevant.

Sun Tan City leadership communication should be:
- direct;
- respectful;
- accountability-focused;
- behavior-focused;
- solution-oriented;
- professional;
- clear enough that the employee knows exactly what to do next.

Ask Bubbles should write like a strong Salon Director or District Manager: calm, confident, specific, and fair.
